int8 GEMM on the NPU against the CPU's own int8 GEMM kernels -- the first clean int8
GEMM number in this project. Every int8 figure before this came from the chained conv
design (ml/bottleneck, layers_conv2_x), whose two kernels each carried a width-32 bug and
which lost to the CPU 5.7-12.75x; the one NPU win on record is bf16 GEMM. This runs the
same upstream whole_array.py design in int8 (--dtype_in i8 --dtype_out i32) at the bf16
sweep's shapes, adds an M-edge sweep with an m-tile control, a tile check, four L1
ceiling probes, and the CPU int8 GEMM baseline this repo never had.
Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1), 2026-09-07, mlir-aie v1.4.2, ironenv (NPU) +
resnet_env (CPU: torch 2.4.1+cpu, onnxruntime 1.22.1). All rows one sitting, 13:14-14:25.
A second Claude Code session was live in this same checkout throughout, working the bf16
FFN thread (its commits 80770d3 and earlier); the process snapshot taken at the start and
end of every NPU batch below found no other python/aiecc/clang process, and no NPU run of
its overlapped these.

WHY THIS EXISTS
===============
This project is named for int8 (Quark XINT8 is the only dtype the VitisAI X1 backend
takes) and XDNA1's 16 TOPS nameplate is an int8 figure. AMD's own device.yaml
(notes_aie2_device_dtypes.log) gives the AIE2 tile 256 int8xint8 MACs/cycle against 128
for bf16 -- so on paper int8 GEMM should run at 2x the bf16 rate that
bf16_matmul_niche_npu.log found to be this NPU's first genuine win. Nobody had measured
it: the int8 numbers in kernels/ are all conv, on a design with since-fixed kernel bugs.
And there was no CPU int8 GEMM baseline to read such a number against -- every CPU int8
number here is ORT QDQ conv. Two questions, then: does int8 GEMM double the bf16 rate on
this design, and does it beat the CPU's own int8 GEMM kernel? On this machine the second
question is the sharper one, because Zen4 has AVX-512 VNNI: the CPU is relatively
strongest exactly at the NPU's headline dtype.

WHAT WAS RUN
============
NPU: kernels/int8_matmul_sweep/npu_matmul_sweep.py drives
~/mlir-aie/programming_examples/basic/matrix_multiplication/whole_array/whole_array.py
(upstream v1.4.2, unmodified) once per row: 4 columns, 10 timed iters after 3 warmup,
tiles m=64/k=64/n=32 unless the row says otherwise. Each row reports the script's own
NPU-time bracket (around kernel.wait()), its end-to-end bracket (around the Python call)
and the GOPS/GFLOPS it derives from the NPU bracket (2MKN / avg). Verification is the
script's own numpy A@B check: for integer dtypes that is np.array_equal, so every int8
PASS below is bit-exact; the bf16->f32 PASS is a 5% / 0.5 tolerance. whole_array needs
M % (m*4) == 0 and (M/m/4) % 2 == 0, so M=128 forces m=16 and M=256 forces m=32 -- those
rows say so, and the m-tile control rows exist because of it.

  python kernels/int8_matmul_sweep/npu_matmul_sweep.py --dtype i8 --iters 10 --warmup 3 \
      --shapes 512x512x512,512x512x1024,512x512x2048,1024x1024x1024,2048x2048x2048,\
               4096x2048x2048,2048x4096x4096,128x4096x4096,256x4096x4096,512x4096x4096,\
               1024x4096x4096,512x4096x4096@16,512x4096x4096@32
  python kernels/int8_matmul_sweep/npu_matmul_sweep.py --dtype bf16 --iters 10 --warmup 3 \
      --shapes 512x512x512,1024x1024x1024,2048x2048x2048,2048x4096x4096,128x4096x4096,\
               256x4096x4096,512x4096x4096,1024x4096x4096,512x4096x4096@16,512x4096x4096@32
  (each row is: whole_array.py --dev npu -M .. -K .. -N .. -m .. -k 64 -n 32 --n-aie-cols 4
   --dtype_in i8 --dtype_out i32 --warmup 3 --iters 10, or bf16/f32)
  Tile check / probes: the same driver with -k 128, -n 64, @128 (m), see below.

CPU: kernels/int8_matmul_sweep/cpu_int8_matmul_sweep.py, resnet_env, 20 timed iters
after 5 warmup, GOPS from the mean (this repo's convention; min is printed too). Two int8
kernels, because this repo has twice been wrong by picking the slower CPU kernel
(conv2x_int8_cpu_baseline.log, the MobileViT splice): torch._int_mm (int8 x int8 ->
int32, torch's own CPU int8 GEMM, 8 threads) and ORT MatMulInteger u8s8 (MLAS -- the
library behind every other CPU int8 number in this repo). Both are checked exactly (int64
reference up to 2^30 MACs, torch-vs-ORT cross-check at every shape); the faster one is the
verdict line. torch bf16 and fp32 matmul are timed in the same run.

  conda activate resnet_env
  python kernels/int8_matmul_sweep/cpu_int8_matmul_sweep.py --iters 20 --warmup 5 \
      --shapes 512x512x512,512x512x1024,512x512x2048,1024x1024x1024,2048x2048x2048,\
               4096x2048x2048,2048x4096x4096,128x4096x4096,256x4096x4096,512x4096x4096,\
               1024x4096x4096

