(ms columns are mean/min over the timed iterations; 'best i8' is the faster of the two int8 kernels)

Two things to read off the CPU table before any comparison. torch._int_mm beats ORT's
MatMulInteger at every shape (1.05-1.68x), so it is the verdict line; every earlier CPU
int8 number in this repo came from MLAS, which for GEMM is the slower of the two -- that
does not reopen the closed conv class (GEMM != conv, and ORT's QLinearConv is a different
MLAS path), it just means "the CPU kernel you lose to" matters here too. And the torch
kernel's mean is far above its min at the big shapes (2048^3: 7.08 ms mean, 4.09 ms min
-- 2427 GOPS by the mean, 4200 by the min), a spread ORT does not show. The verdict below
uses the mean, as every CPU number in this repo does; the min-based figure is quoted
wherever it would change the reading.

TABLE 1 -- NPU int8 vs NPU bf16, same sitting, default tiles (m=64 k=64 n=32)
=============================================================================
      MxKxN         m    int8 GOPS   bf16 GFLOPS   int8/bf16
  -----------------------------------------------------------
   512x512x512     64       982.52        846.40      1.16x
  1024x1024x1024   64      2743.72       1875.30      1.46x
  2048x2048x2048   64      2373.66       1775.65      1.34x
  2048x4096x4096   64      2047.66       1801.18      1.14x
   128x4096x4096   16*      585.31        595.42      0.98x
   256x4096x4096   32*     1041.28       1034.96      1.01x
   512x4096x4096   64      1875.49       1671.94      1.12x
  1024x4096x4096   64      1956.97       1779.78      1.10x
  (* m forced by M; see TABLE 2)

The 2x the MAC count promises does not appear anywhere: 1.10-1.46x at the square and
2048-class shapes, and parity at the forced small tiles. Whatever bounds this design at
the default tile is dtype-independent -- the int32 and f32 output tiles are the same 4
bytes per element, so the per-k-step read-modify-write of the output tile and the
per-tile FIFO handshakes cost int8 exactly what they cost bf16.

TABLE 2 -- M-edge at K=N=4096 (LLM prefill length), with the m-tile control
============================================================================
      MxKxN         m    int8 GOPS   bf16 GFLOPS     note
  ------------------------------------------------------------------
   128x4096x4096   16       585.31        595.42     m forced by M
   256x4096x4096   32      1041.28       1034.96     m forced by M
   512x4096x4096   64      1875.49       1671.94
  1024x4096x4096   64      1956.97       1779.78
  2048x4096x4096   64      2047.66       1801.18
   512x4096x4096   16       609.93        631.46     control: same M, forced m
   512x4096x4096   32      1045.84       1057.94     control: same M, forced m

The control rows settle what the M-edge rows would otherwise have overstated: M=128 at
m=16 (585) is M=512 at m=16 (610), and M=256 at m=32 (1041) is M=512 at m=32 (1046).
Throughput tracks the tile row size m -- close to linearly, 16 -> 32 -> 64 is 610 -> 1046
-> 1875 -- and not the token count M at all. A short prefill is slow on this design
because whole_array's 4 rows x 2 transfer blocks force m = M/8 at small M, not because the
hardware does less work per second on it. Whether a differently tiled design could keep
m=64 at M=128 is not tested here.

TABLE 3 -- int8 tile check at 2048^3 (what int8's half-size A/B tiles buy)
==========================================================================
     m    k    n     L1 A/B/C double-buffered     int8 GOPS    vs default
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    64   64   32     16 + 8 + 16 KB = 40 KB        2322.61      (re-run; 2373.66 in the sweep)
    64  128   32     16 + 8 + 16 KB = 40 KB        2788.00       1.20x
   128   64   32     16 + 8 + 32 KB = 56 KB        3192.69       1.37x
    64   64   64     16 + 16 + 32 KB = 64 KB       4603.26       1.98x

n=64 doubles the int8 rate, bit-exact. The reason is not the MAC count: every extra
column of n halves the number of times the whole A matrix is re-streamed from DDR
(whole_array reloads A once per N/(n*4) column block, and re-streams B once per M/(m*4)
row block), so total DDR traffic per GEMM goes as MKN/4 * (1/n + 1/m). m=64/n=32 is
0.047 per MAC; n=64 is 0.031 (-33%), m=128 is 0.039 (-17%); the measured gains are
larger than the traffic reduction alone, so per-tile fixed cost shrinks too. k=128 only
halves the output-tile read-modify-write per MAC and buys the least. The same n=64 tile
in bf16 is 16 + 16 + 32 KB with a 2-byte input -- A and B tiles twice the bytes -- which
is why bf16 cannot follow (TABLE 4).

TABLE 4 -- L1 ceiling probes at 2048^3 (do the bigger tiles compile at all?)
===========================================================================
All four fail in aiecc before anything runs, with the same error --
  'aie.tile' op Basic sequential allocation failed
-- and the allocator's own dump (RAW section above) shows the double-buffered A/B/C
tiles plus the 0xD00 (3,328 B) stack against the tile's 65,536 B:

  dtype   m    k    n    A/B/C tile bytes (x2 each)   needs      result
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  int8    64   64   32   4096 / 2048 / 8192            32,000    PASS (the default)
  int8    64   64   64   4096 / 4096 / 16384           52,480    PASS (TABLE 3, 4603 GOPS)
  int8   128   64   64   8192 / 4096 / 32768           93,440    FAIL
  int8    64  128   64   8192 / 8192 / 16384           68,864    FAIL (over by 3,328 B)
  bf16    64   64   32   8192 / 4096 / 8192            44,288    PASS (the default)
  bf16    64   64   64   8192 / 8192 / 16384           68,864    FAIL (over by 3,328 B)
  bf16   128   64   32   16384 / 4096 / 16384          77,056    FAIL

bf16's n=64 misses the tile by exactly the stack. int8's default tile sits at 32 KB of
64 KB, which is what leaves it room to reach n=64; bf16's default is already at 44 KB.

