THE FINDING
===========
1. At the tile whole_array ships with, the NPU's headline dtype loses to the CPU's own
   int8 GEMM kernel almost everywhere: 1.02-1.41x behind at the square and 2048-class
   shapes, 2.7-4.9x behind at short prefill (M <= 256), one 1.21x win at 1024^3. In the
   same sitting the same design in bf16 beats CPU bf16 by 1.13-1.35x at every shape with
   M >= 512. So the plain reading of the first two batches is: on this machine, bf16 is
   the niche and int8 is not -- because the CPU is relatively strongest (AVX-512 VNNI,
   torch's kernel at 2.1-2.9 TOPS by the mean, 4.0-4.2 at its best) at exactly the
   dtype the NPU is sold on, and the design's default tile gives int8 only 1.1-1.5x the
   bf16 rate, not 2x. The costs that bound it there are dtype-independent: the whole A
   matrix is re-streamed from DDR once per column block and B once per row block, each
   k-step handshakes two tiles through two FIFO levels, and the output tile it
   read-modify-writes is 4 bytes per element for i32 and f32 alike.

2. What int8 has that bf16 does not is L1 headroom. Its A and B tiles are half the
   bytes, so the default tile uses 32 KB of the 64 KB and n=64 fits at 52 KB; bf16 sits at
   44 KB by default and its n=64 misses the tile by exactly the 3,328 B stack (TABLE 4).
   n=64 halves the number of times A is re-streamed and the int8 rate doubles, bit-exact
   (TABLE 3, TABLE 5): 4448-4607 GOPS at the 2048-class shapes, 3160-3282 at the
   K=N=4096 shapes with M >= 512. That is 2.5x the bf16 rate of the same sitting at
   2048^3, and the highest ops rate any single dispatch has reached in this project.

3. The verdict, by this repo's mean-based convention (TABLE 6): with n=64, NPU int8
   beats the CPU's best int8 kernel by 1.10-1.83x at every shape with M >= 512 and
   N >= 2048, and still loses at 512^3 (2.1x), 512x512x1024 (1.2x) and short prefill
   (M=128: 2.6x, M=256: 1.5x). The margin is real but thin at the largest shapes: read
   against the CPU kernel's best-case (min) time, the 2048-class wins hold at 1.06-1.10x
   and the K=N=4096 rows become a 1.13-1.24x CPU win. int8 GEMM is therefore a second
   genuine NPU niche on this machine, in absolute terms about twice as fast as the bf16
   one -- but only with the tile the dtype's smaller footprint buys, and relative to its
   own CPU competitor no wider than the bf16 niche is relative to CPU bf16 at the
   largest shapes (1.18x vs 1.19x at 2048x4096x4096).

4. The M-edge is a tile artifact, not a hardware property. Throughput tracks m, not M
   (TABLE 2: M=128 at m=16 equals M=512 at m=16 to within 6%, at both dtypes), and n=64
   lifts the small-M rows by the same 1.8x it lifts everything else without changing
   which side wins them. On this design a prefill shorter than ~512 tokens at
   d_model=4096 loses at either dtype because whole_array's 4 rows x 2 transfer blocks
   force m = M/8; a differently tiled design is the only thing that could change that.

5. The mechanism is a DDR-traffic mechanism, and it says what to try next. Total
   traffic per GEMM on this design goes as MKN/4 * (1/n + 1/m) -- n=64 cuts it a third,
   m=128 a sixth, and measured gains exceed both, so per-tile fixed cost is in there too.
   bf16 is stuck at the default tile by 3,328 B. Single-buffering the C output FIFO
   (depths=[1] in whole_array.py's C join -- the same one-line change that got
   bottleneck.py to 56x56, bottleneck_w56_npu.log) would free 16 KB and let bf16 take
   n=64. NOT done here: whole_array.py is the shared upstream file the other live
   session is running its FFN measurements through, and editing it under them would
   silently change their numbers. It is the obvious follow-up: if bf16 gains at n=64
   what int8 gained, the bf16 niche roughly doubles too.

NOT TESTED / CAVEATS
====================
- Same sitting, one machine: every number above is from 13:14-14:25 on Desktop 2. CPU
  bf16 came in at 1310-1580 GFLOPS here against 1100-1360 in bf16_matmul_niche_npu.log
  earlier today (~15% higher), which is why the bf16 NPU margin reads 1.13-1.35x in
  TABLE 6 rather than that log's 1.18-1.78x -- drift on the CPU side, not a new NPU
  result. Compare within this log.
- The CPU verdict kernel (torch._int_mm) has a large mean/min spread at the big shapes
  (2048^3: 7.08 ms mean, 4.09 ms min) that ORT's MatMulInteger does not; the verdict uses
  the mean like every other CPU number here, and the min-based reading is given beside
  it. Nothing was done to pin threads or frequency on either side.
- ORT MatMulInteger (MLAS, u8s8) is 1.05-1.68x slower than torch's int8 kernel for GEMM
  on this machine. Every earlier CPU int8 number in this repo is MLAS (QLinearConv); GEMM
  is not conv and this does not reopen the closed int8 conv class -- it is one more case
  of the CPU kernel choice moving the verdict. ORT s8s8 (int8 activations) fell to 45.6
  GOPS in the probe before this log's runs -- an unvectorized MLAS path -- and was not
  used.
- int8 -> int32 output only. A real quantized layer requantizes back to int8 (XINT8's
  power-of-two scales make that a shift); that epilogue is not measured. Upstream's
  i8->i8 kernel path accumulates across K in an int8 C buffer and is unusable for K > k;
  i8->i16 wraps at these input ranges. The 4-byte output tile is part of why the default
  tile is dtype-blind.
- Not tried: n=64 for bf16 via the C-FIFO depth change above; --n-aie-cols other than 4;
  int16 (the design's default dtype); k=128 combined with n=64 (does not fit, TABLE 4);
  b_col_maj/c_col_maj; any shape past K=N=4096.
- whole_array.py's own numpy int64 reference is what made the 4096-class int8 rows
  take 3-13 minutes of wall each; the NPU part of every row is milliseconds.
